INT. CONVERTED SCHOOL BUS BEDROOM - NIGHT

A decommissioned yellow bus, parked on cinder blocks in an overgrown field. Inside, the seats are gone. A mattress on the floor, a milk crate for a nightstand, string lights stapled to the ceiling. A cassette recorder sits on the crate, a red light blinking.

No one is here. Rain ticks on the roof.

The recorder clicks and begins to play. A girl's voice, young, bright, trying hard to sound older.

ADAEZE (V.O.)
(recorded, tinny)
Test, test. Okay. It's July the ninth. I'm fourteen. I'm recording this for me. For the older me. If you're hearing it, you're, like, thirty or something.

The bus door creaks open. A gust of wet air.

ADAEZE NWOSU, 17, a duffel bag slung over one shoulder, a high-school graduation tassel still pinned to her jacket, climbs the steps, pushing wet braids from her face. She does not see the recorder. She drops the duffel on the mattress and sits.

ADAEZE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded)
So here are my predictions. Number one. You live somewhere with a window that opens. Number two. You're not scared of anything.

Adaeze stops. Looks at the crate. Looks at the recorder.

ADAEZE
(whispering)
No way.

ADAEZE (V.O.)
(recorded)
Number three. You've called Mom.

The words hang. Adaeze sits very still.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
(to the recorder)
I haven't.

ADAEZE (V.O.)
(recorded)
Number four. You've forgiven Dad.

She turns the volume down with a finger. The room goes soft.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
(quietly)
That one's harder.

She pulls her knees up. Opens the duffel. Takes out a folded letter with a college seal. She turns it over twice. Tucks it back in.

ADAEZE (V.O.)
(recorded)
Number five. You still have this tape, which means you didn't throw away anything good.

She picks up the recorder. Holds it like a bird.

ADAEZE
(softly)
I kept it.

She rewinds a few seconds. Plays it again.

ADAEZE (V.O.)
(recorded)
Number five. You still have this tape...

Adaeze presses record. The little red light glows. She clears her throat, speaks into it, steady and careful.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
Test, test. It's June the second. I'm seventeen. I'm recording this for the girl I used to be.

She stops. Her voice cracks, then settles.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
Here's what happened. I got in. I'm going. I'm scared. And I called Mom.

She laughs, and it breaks, and she leans her forehead against the cold metal wall of the bus.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
She said you were right about number three.

Rain eases. The string lights shiver. She holds the recorder close to her mouth and whispers the last line, so that only the tape and the bus can hear.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
Number four: I'm working on it.

She clicks the recorder off.
